265
00:10:21,920 --> 00:10:23,840
if you did what you were supposed to do,

266
00:10:23,840 --> 00:10:26,960
and here's the nice thing about this harsh punishment.

267
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:29,200
Since I only impose it if you didn't live up

268
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:31,480
to the agreement, you have a great way

269
00:10:31,480 --> 00:10:33,720
to make sure you never suffer the punishment.

270
00:10:35,080 --> 00:10:38,440
So I execute anyone who I catch playing Tetris on the job.

271
00:10:39,480 --> 00:10:41,520
But there's a really simple solution

272
00:10:41,520 --> 00:10:44,000
if you don't want to get executed.

273
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:45,760
What's the simple solution?

274
00:10:45,760 --> 00:10:47,560
Don't play Tetris.

275
00:10:47,560 --> 00:10:49,600
Right, and as long as there's no ambiguity

276
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:51,960
about whether you played Tetris or not,

277
00:10:51,960 --> 00:10:54,120
well, really easy.

278
00:10:54,120 --> 00:10:57,280
Okay, so you can often infer behavior from outcomes,

279
00:10:57,280 --> 00:11:00,160
sometimes perfectly, especially in a homework problem,

280
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:02,120
but in the real world, you can again often get

281
00:11:02,120 --> 00:11:04,240
a pretty good idea about what's going on,

282
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:06,280
like, well, I know how smart you are,

283
00:11:06,280 --> 00:11:10,160
and I know how well you did on this test that I gave you,

284
00:11:10,160 --> 00:11:13,120
so I can then back out how much time you put into this.

285
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:16,160
Not with certainty, but you can get a pretty good idea

286
00:11:16,160 --> 00:11:17,720
about how someone was doing.

287
00:11:17,720 --> 00:11:19,320
And again, of course, there are many things

288
00:11:19,320 --> 00:11:21,400
that when you see them, if the person said,

289
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:24,520
I tried really hard, you might say, no, you didn't.

290
00:11:24,520 --> 00:11:28,760
So if someone, you know, has,

291
00:11:28,760 --> 00:11:32,280
on a residential street, has crashed a car

292
00:11:32,280 --> 00:11:34,320
into somebody's garage, and they say,

293
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:36,200
look, I was driving perfectly fine,

294
00:11:36,200 --> 00:11:38,560
and the car just took over,

295
00:11:38,560 --> 00:11:41,640
and the wheel turned all on its own, and rammed,

296
00:11:41,640 --> 00:11:43,680
and the accelerator went down to full,

297
00:11:43,680 --> 00:11:46,480
and ran that garage, like, hmm,

298
00:11:46,480 --> 00:11:49,360
that sounds like a complete lie.

299
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:53,680
And this couldn't happen if you were doing

300
00:11:53,680 --> 00:11:54,960
what you're supposed to do.

301
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:56,560
If you were doing what you're supposed to do,

302
00:11:56,560 --> 00:11:59,320
then you would have stayed on the road

303
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:01,880
at a reasonable speed, and this garage door

304
00:12:01,880 --> 00:12:02,880
would not be destroyed.

305
00:12:02,880 --> 00:12:05,480
So, you can do it that way.

306
00:12:05,480 --> 00:12:07,680
All right, so anyway, so if you can perfectly infer

307
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:10,480
behavior from outcomes, then information asymmetries

308
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:14,400
don't make very little difference, really no difference,

309
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:17,760
because there's a bunch of things that you don't know,

310
00:12:17,760 --> 00:12:19,360
that there's asymmetric information,

311
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:23,360
but you know enough to figure out what happened,

312
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:25,200
and as long as you know enough to figure out what happened,

313
00:12:25,200 --> 00:12:26,240
the fact that you don't know everything

314
00:12:26,240 --> 00:12:27,280
isn't very important.

315
00:12:28,160 --> 00:12:29,880
Okay, all right.

316
00:12:30,760 --> 00:12:32,840
But inferences from behavior and outcomes

317
00:12:32,840 --> 00:12:35,160
are, in the real world, of course, less than perfect,

318
00:12:35,160 --> 00:12:37,240
so the moral hazard problem does persist to some degree.

319
00:12:37,240 --> 00:12:39,480
So, you know, maybe the person driving the car

320
00:12:39,480 --> 00:12:42,160
had a sudden blackout, and they've never had a blackout

321
00:12:42,160 --> 00:12:44,760
in their life before, so they had no way of knowing

322
00:12:44,760 --> 00:12:45,920
that there would ever be a blackout,

323
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:47,120
and that's what happened.

324
00:12:47,120 --> 00:12:48,680
All right, so that could occur.

325
00:12:49,640 --> 00:12:52,680
All right, now moral hazard is not an efficiency problem

326
00:12:52,680 --> 00:12:54,640
if the agents are risk neutral, of course.

327
00:12:54,640 --> 00:12:57,800
Then you just live, then you don't have any insurance,

328
00:12:57,800 --> 00:12:59,800
and you just let people live with the risk,

329
00:12:59,800 --> 00:13:00,920
and if people live with the risk,

330
00:13:00,920 --> 00:13:03,480
then there's not gonna be any issue.

331
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:06,280
So imagine a risk neutral CEO.

332
00:13:07,240 --> 00:13:09,680
There is a really simple way that you can go

333
00:13:09,680 --> 00:13:11,160
and handle the problem of good incentives

334
00:13:11,160 --> 00:13:13,440
to the risk neutral CEO, and then sell them

335
00:13:13,440 --> 00:13:14,320
the whole company.

336
00:13:14,320 --> 00:13:18,400
So you turn the company into a sole proprietorship,

337
00:13:18,400 --> 00:13:21,840
and then, once you own the entire thing,

338
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:25,120
if it is, you know, the only reason why you wouldn't go

339
00:13:25,120 --> 00:13:26,360
and put in a million dollars worth of,

340
00:13:26,360 --> 00:13:28,320
or if there's something that will make the company

341
00:13:28,320 --> 00:13:30,560
worth a million more, the only reason why you wouldn't

342
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:32,720
put in the effort to do that is if the effort

343
00:13:32,720 --> 00:13:37,720
subjectively costs you more than, what?

344
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:42,360
More than, yeah, so like, you know, you get out of bed,

345
00:13:42,360 --> 00:13:44,280
and you can save the company a million dollars.

346
00:13:44,280 --> 00:13:46,040
Why don't you get out of bed if you're the sole owner

347
00:13:46,040 --> 00:13:47,040
of the company?

348
00:13:47,040 --> 00:13:48,200
Staying in bed's worth more than a million dollars.

349
00:13:48,200 --> 00:13:50,360
Yeah, staying in bed's worth more than a million dollars.

350
00:13:50,360 --> 00:13:51,200
Could that ever be?

351
00:13:51,200 --> 00:13:53,760
I mean, once you're a billionaire, I would imagine.

352
00:13:53,760 --> 00:13:55,120
Yeah, seems like it.

353
00:13:55,120 --> 00:13:57,520
Okay, all right, so the idea is then you would exert

354
00:13:57,520 --> 00:13:59,960
management efforts if and only if the expected gain

355
00:13:59,960 --> 00:14:02,360
exceeds the expected effort cost.

356
00:14:02,360 --> 00:14:05,320
So, and in Landsberg, he talks about the problem

357
00:14:05,320 --> 00:14:08,080
of the moral hazard involved in splitting a check.

358
00:14:08,080 --> 00:14:10,760
Right, so you go out to dinner with 10 people,

359
00:14:10,760 --> 00:14:12,160
you don't even like each other very much,

360
00:14:12,160 --> 00:14:14,200
just to make the problem more interesting,

361
00:14:14,200 --> 00:14:17,280
and then there's the question of how do we split the check?

362
00:14:17,280 --> 00:14:20,080
Well, the person, like one convenient answer is

363
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:22,520
we all just go and put in an equal amount.

364
00:14:23,520 --> 00:14:25,600
The problem with that, of course, especially if people

365
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:27,560
know that's gonna be the rule,

366
00:14:27,560 --> 00:14:28,600
suppose you know that's the rule,

367
00:14:28,600 --> 00:14:30,680
then what does this lead everybody to do?

368
00:14:30,680 --> 00:14:31,520
Free ride.

369
00:14:31,520 --> 00:14:33,200
Free ride, how?

370
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:35,640
Order expensive, order lots of expensive stuff.

371
00:14:35,640 --> 00:14:38,120
So yeah, there's that approach.

372
00:14:38,120 --> 00:14:41,000
Of course, the opposite end is have everybody go

373
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:42,960
and pay for exactly what they paid for,

374
00:14:42,960 --> 00:14:45,800
so everyone pays based upon exactly what they bought,

375
00:14:45,800 --> 00:14:47,880
but that requires a very extensive monitoring,

376
00:14:47,880 --> 00:14:50,440
that seems like, that's pretty hard, that's quite hard.

377
00:14:50,440 --> 00:14:53,560
And then Landsberg comes up with a really weird contract.

378
00:14:53,560 --> 00:14:56,520
Here's the contract where everyone pays the full check.

379
00:14:57,600 --> 00:15:00,320
So that's computationally very simple, right?

380
00:15:00,320 --> 00:15:03,960
Everyone pays, if the group check comes to $800,

381
00:15:03,960 --> 00:15:06,480
everyone pays $800.

382
00:15:06,480 --> 00:15:08,960
Why would anyone go to a restaurant where everyone

383
00:15:08,960 --> 00:15:11,400
has to pay the full check if they're in a group?

384
00:15:11,400 --> 00:15:13,280
And Landsberg's answer is, well,

385
00:15:13,280 --> 00:15:15,720
restaurants would pay you to come in.

386
00:15:15,720 --> 00:15:17,720
Restaurants would actually go and say,

387
00:15:17,720 --> 00:15:22,720
look, here is a $770 voucher for every person who comes in,

388
00:15:24,840 --> 00:15:26,960
as long as you're part of a group of 10 people

389
00:15:26,960 --> 00:15:28,360
and you don't like each other.

390
00:15:29,800 --> 00:15:32,200
All right, so that would be a possibility.

391
00:15:32,200 --> 00:15:34,720
Again, the idea there is that at the margin,

392
00:15:34,720 --> 00:15:37,520
if you're thinking, should I go and order this $10 dessert,

393
00:15:37,520 --> 00:15:39,480
if you know that you're paying the full check,

394
00:15:39,480 --> 00:15:42,600
you will order the dessert as long as you value that dessert

395
00:15:42,600 --> 00:15:46,440
at $10 dessert more than $10,

396
00:15:46,440 --> 00:15:49,440
because you know this will raise my check by $10.

397
00:15:49,440 --> 00:15:51,240
It'll also raise the checks of a bunch of other people

398
00:15:51,240 --> 00:15:52,440
that I don't care about by $10,

399
00:15:52,440 --> 00:15:54,400
but I don't care about them, so too bad.

400
00:15:54,400 --> 00:15:55,840
I guess if you truly dislike them,

401
00:15:55,840 --> 00:15:58,960
you might actually enjoy impoverishing them.

402
00:15:58,960 --> 00:16:01,640
Just say a bunch of people where you're like,

403
00:16:01,640 --> 00:16:03,960
I could take them or leave them, whatever.

404
00:16:03,960 --> 00:16:06,880
So why you're eating with these people is a fair question.

405
00:16:06,880 --> 00:16:10,200
All right, so anyway, this Landsberg thought experiment

406
00:16:10,200 --> 00:16:12,720
is the same idea behind selling the whole company

407
00:16:12,720 --> 00:16:16,200
to the CEO so that you turn every company

408
00:16:16,200 --> 00:16:17,400
into sole proprietorship.

409
00:16:18,280 --> 00:16:22,000
Now, another solutionable hazard,

410
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,200
and this is one that is 100 times more realistic

411
00:16:24,200 --> 00:16:26,760
than Landsberg's one, maybe even a million times

412
00:16:26,760 --> 00:16:28,560
more realistic than Landsberg's one.

413
00:16:29,840 --> 00:16:31,360
It's one, as I think I mentioned already,

414
00:16:31,360 --> 00:16:32,760
Robin Hanson in his dissertation,

415
00:16:32,760 --> 00:16:34,680
he had a whole chapter on this idea.

416
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:37,240
It's one of these ideas that is common sense and it's great

417
00:16:37,240 --> 00:16:39,320
and still hardly anybody does anything with it.

418
00:16:39,320 --> 00:16:43,520
In a way, it's too obvious, but it's so doable

419
00:16:43,520 --> 00:16:46,600
that it's frustrating that it gets a little attention.

420
00:16:46,600 --> 00:16:50,120
So here is another remedy from lower hazard,

421
00:16:50,120 --> 00:16:53,640
and that is for a contractual punishment.

422
00:16:59,520 --> 00:17:01,360
So here you solve the Maranza problem,

423
00:17:01,360 --> 00:17:05,920
not in this weird way of having everyone pay the entire check.

424
00:17:05,920 --> 00:17:09,160
You solve it by saying, look, if I,

425
00:17:09,160 --> 00:17:12,760
the following things count as cheating on this deal.

426
00:17:12,760 --> 00:17:14,520
If I'm ever caught doing these things,

427
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:16,520
I owe you a lot of money.

428
00:17:16,520 --> 00:17:19,400
I owe you more than the money that I took from you

429
00:17:19,400 --> 00:17:20,840
by cheating.

430
00:17:21,960 --> 00:17:23,680
So for example, you have a contract that says

431
00:17:23,680 --> 00:17:25,760
if the boss ever catches you playing Tetris,

432
00:17:25,760 --> 00:17:28,200
you owe the boss $5,000.

433
00:17:28,200 --> 00:17:30,000
And you sign that contract.

434
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:31,560
Now you might say, I don't know, that contract

435
00:17:31,560 --> 00:17:32,960
seems pretty dangerous.

436
00:17:32,960 --> 00:17:35,440
Maybe he's just gonna go and falsely accuse me.

437
00:17:35,440 --> 00:17:36,800
All right, well, if that's your concern,

438
00:17:36,800 --> 00:17:39,000
then don't sign the contract.

439
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:43,600
But you would say, fine, I'll go and agree to a $500 fine.

440
00:17:43,600 --> 00:17:46,880
So something that, like, any time that you arrange it

441
00:17:46,880 --> 00:17:50,280
so that it is, the fine is large enough

442
00:17:50,280 --> 00:17:52,200
that it's not worth it for you to do it.

443
00:17:52,200 --> 00:17:54,200
And then, of course, the employer doesn't need

444
00:17:54,200 --> 00:17:56,520
to put a lot of resources into monitoring you.

445
00:17:56,520 --> 00:17:59,360
They only need to do an occasional random audit.

446
00:17:59,360 --> 00:18:00,680
They can just have some dice.

447
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:02,200
And if the dice all come up the wrong way,

448
00:18:02,200 --> 00:18:03,760
today's audit day.

449
00:18:03,760 --> 00:18:05,400
Of course, you don't tell people that.

450
00:18:05,400 --> 00:18:08,720
You just go and do a surprise inspection.

451
00:18:08,720 --> 00:18:11,240
And then you find out what's really going on.

452
00:18:11,240 --> 00:18:12,560
All right, so I think, remember, like,

453
00:18:12,560 --> 00:18:15,600
I think it was Tetris actually had a boss coming button

454
00:18:15,600 --> 00:18:16,920
where you click it and then it's,

455
00:18:16,920 --> 00:18:18,560
it hides the Tetris instantly.

456
00:18:18,560 --> 00:18:21,040
There isn't even, like, a minimized Tetris icon.

457
00:18:21,040 --> 00:18:22,960
It just completely disappears.

458
00:18:22,960 --> 00:18:24,920
So you could do that.

459
00:18:24,920 --> 00:18:27,480
But anyway, so the idea is if the less informed

460
00:18:27,480 --> 00:18:31,320
can pay to observe the more informed,

461
00:18:31,320 --> 00:18:34,360
then they can enforce good behavior at a low cost

462
00:18:34,360 --> 00:18:38,600
with random monitoring combined with severe punishment.

463
00:18:38,600 --> 00:18:39,720
Severe punishment.

464
00:18:39,720 --> 00:18:42,600
So it can't be heads I win, tails I break even.

465
00:18:42,600 --> 00:18:45,960
In that case, everybody's going to want to cheat.

466
00:18:45,960 --> 00:18:48,680
But on the other hand, if it's heads I win,

467
00:18:48,680 --> 00:18:53,520
and tails I go and I lose three times my gain from cheating,

468
00:18:53,520 --> 00:18:56,680
then the expected gain from cheating is negative.

469
00:18:56,680 --> 00:18:58,760
And in which case, the boss may need to put in

470
00:18:58,760 --> 00:19:01,200
very few resources in actually monitoring you.

471
00:19:02,640 --> 00:19:04,880
Right, and that is the idea.

472
00:19:04,880 --> 00:19:07,960
Now, there are many examples of how this can be done.

473
00:19:07,960 --> 00:19:09,720
You may say, well, isn't it already done?

474
00:19:09,720 --> 00:19:11,680
Well, so to some extent,

475
00:19:11,680 --> 00:19:13,160
but as I think I already mentioned,

476
00:19:13,160 --> 00:19:16,200
in all countries where the law descends

477
00:19:16,200 --> 00:19:17,880
from that of England,

478
00:19:17,880 --> 00:19:20,720
there is a rule saying that you cannot agree

479
00:19:20,720 --> 00:19:22,600
to a punitive punishment.

480
00:19:22,600 --> 00:19:26,480
So you can contractually agree to compensate someone

481
00:19:26,480 --> 00:19:28,200
for what you have cost them,

482
00:19:28,200 --> 00:19:30,560
but you can't contractually agree to more.

483
00:19:31,760 --> 00:19:33,560
Right, and then Robin points out,

484
00:19:33,560 --> 00:19:35,440
well, that's highly inefficient.

485
00:19:35,440 --> 00:19:38,640
Essentially, it's saying, you can sign a contract saying,

486
00:19:38,640 --> 00:19:40,360
heads I win, tails I break even.

487
00:19:41,440 --> 00:19:42,880
You can't sign a contract saying,

488
00:19:42,880 --> 00:19:45,280
heads I win and tails I lose.

489
00:19:45,280 --> 00:19:47,680
And of course, to convince people not to take a risk,

490
00:19:47,680 --> 00:19:49,560
you really need to put in that data,

491
00:19:49,560 --> 00:19:51,760
that actual benefit that you're getting.

492
00:19:51,760 --> 00:19:56,600
That actual bad outcome, or else it is a good gamble.

493
00:19:56,600 --> 00:19:57,440
Yes.

494
00:19:57,440 --> 00:19:58,280
Isn't this what a bond is?

495
00:19:58,280 --> 00:20:23,280
You put it up the money beforehand.


